# Line endings and text encoding, fixed before there is content to convert.
#
# Contributors arrive from three operating systems. Without a stated rule the tree
# accumulates mixed line endings, and the first casualty is any test whose input
# has to be byte-exact, which in a project that parses protocol frames is most of
# the interesting ones.
#
# This is not the same rule as the Unicode guard in
# .github/workflows/unicode-guard.yml, and the two are easy to confuse because
# both are about bytes in text. The guard refuses bidirectional and invisible
# control characters, which is text that renders differently from how it parses:
# bytes that lie. This file is about bytes changing under the contributor between
# one checkout and the next. Neither covers the other, and a tree can fail either
# one on its own.

# Every text file is normalised to LF in the repository and checked out with LF on
# every platform.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto` alone is the part that does the work here. With
# `text=auto` alone the repository stays clean and a checkout on Windows still
# produces CRLF working files, which is what made `gofmt` and `golangci-lint run`
# report every file in this tree as unformatted on that platform while the same
# command passed on the runner, for a reason having nothing to do with the change
# being made. Contributors are not asked to set core.autocrlf to work around it,
# because a rule that lives in each contributor's local git config is a rule no
# clone carries.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one exception, and the reason for it.
#
# Go's convention puts test inputs under testdata/. Those are bytes a test asserts
# against, so a file whose line endings were rewritten on the way in or out is a
# test that passes against the wrong input, or fails for a reason no one can see in
# a diff. `-text` turns normalisation off entirely for that tree: the bytes that
# were committed are the bytes that are checked out, on every platform.
#
# It costs readable diffs on those files, which is the right trade. A fixture whose
# diff is readable is a fixture whose bytes are negotiable.
testdata/** -text
